  <content>
  <section><title>Solve by Using Perfect Squares and Completing the Square </title>
<para>Solve \(2x^2+5x+2=0\) by <term data-schema-version="“1.0”" data-store="glossary-tooltip">completing the square</term>.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Divide all terms by the leading coefficient. 
  \(\frac{2x^2+5x+2}{2}=\frac{0}{2}\) 
  \(x^2+\frac{5}{2}x+1=0\) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Move the constant to the right side of the equation. 
  \(x^2+\frac{5}{2}x=-1\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Complete the square and add to each side: \((\frac{5}{2} \times \frac{1}{2})^2=\frac{25}{16}\). 
  \(x^2+\frac{5}{2}x+\frac{25}{16}=-1+\frac{25}{16}\) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Simplify.  
  (Hint: create a common denominator before adding.) 
  \(x^2+\frac{5}{2}x+\frac{25}{16}=-\frac{16}{16}+\frac{25}{16}\)
  \((x+\frac{5}{4})^2=\frac{9}{16}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Take the square root of each side. 
  \(\sqrt{(x+\frac{5}{4})^2}= \sqrt{\frac{9}{16}}\) 
  \(x+\frac{5}{4}= \pm \frac{3}{4}\) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Write two equations and solve. 
  \(x+\frac{5}{4}=\frac{3}{4}\)         \(x+\frac{5}{4}=-\frac{3}{4}\)
  \(x=-\frac{2}{4}=-\frac{1}{2}\)   \(x=-\frac{8}{4}=-2\)</para>
<title>Try It: Solve by Using Perfect Squares and Completing the Square</title>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para>Solve \(4x^2+36x+37=0\).</para>
  <para>Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to solve using completing the square:</para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Divide all terms by the leading coefficient. 
    \(\frac{4x^2+36x+37}{4}=\frac{0}{4}\) 
    \(x^2+9x+\frac{37}{4}=0\) </para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Move the constant to the right side of the equation. 
    \(x^2+9x=-\frac{37}{4}\) </para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Complete the square and add to each side: \((9 \times \frac{1}{2})^2=\frac{81}{4}\). 
    \(x^2+9x+\frac{81}{4}=-\frac{37}{4}+\frac{81}{4}\) </para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - Simplify.  
    \((x+\frac{9}{2})^2=11\) </para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 5</emphasis> - Take the square root of each side. 
    \(\sqrt{(x+\frac{9}{2})^2}=\sqrt{11}\) 
    \((x+\frac{9}{2})= \pm \sqrt{11}\) </para>
  <para><emphasis>Step 6</emphasis> - Write two equations and solve. 
    \(x+\frac{9}{2}=\sqrt{11}\)       \(x+\frac{9}{2}=-\sqrt{11}\)
    \(x=-\frac{9}{2}+\sqrt{11}\)       \(x=-\frac{9}{2}-\sqrt{11}\)</para>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
